nk-text>Add to Mendeley</span></button><div class="Social u-display-inline-block" id=social><div class="popover social-popover" id=social-popover><div id=popover-trigger-social-popover><button class="button-link u-margin-s-right button-link-primary" aria-expanded=false aria-haspopup=true type=button><svg role=img focusable=false viewBox="0 0 128 128" height=16 width=16 class="icon icon-share" aria-label=Share><path d="m9e1 112c-6.62 0-12-5.38-12-12s5.38-12 12-12 12 5.38 12 12-5.38 12-12 12zm-66-36c-6.62 0-12-5.38-12-12s5.38-12 12-12 12 5.38 12 12-5.38 12-12 12zm66-6e1c6.62 0 12 5.38 12 12s-5.38 12-12 12-12-5.38-12-12 5.38-12 12-12zm0 62c-6.56 0-12.44 2.9-16.48 7.48l-28.42-15.28c0.58-1.98 0.9-4.04 0.9-6.2s-0.32-4.22-0.9-6.2l28.42-15.28c4.04 4.58 9.92 7.48 16.48 7.48 12.14 0 22-9.86 22-22s-9.86-22-22-22-22 9.86-22 22c0 1.98 0.28 3.9 0.78 5.72l-28.64 15.38c-4.02-4.34-9.76-7.1-16.14-7.1-12.14 0-22 9.86-22 22s9.86 22 22 22c6.38 0 12.12-2.76 16.14-7.12l28.64 15.38c-0.5 1.84-0.78 3.76-0.78 5.74 0 12.14 9.86 22 22 22s22-9.86 22-22-9.86-22-22-22z"></path></svg><span class=button-link-text>Share</span></button></div></div></div><div class="ExportCitation u-display-inline-block" id=export-citation><div class="popover export-citation-popover" id=export-citation-popover><div id=popover-trigger-export-citation-popover><button class="button-link button-link-primary" aria-expanded=false aria-haspopup=true type=button><svg role=img focusable=false viewBox="0 0 106 128" height=16 width=16 class="icon icon-cited-by-66" aria-label="Cited By"><path xmlns=http://www.w3.org/2000/svg d="m2 58.78v47.22h44v-42h-34v-5.22c0-18.5 17.08-26.78 34-26.78v-1e1c-25.9 0-44 15.12-44 36.78zm1e2 -26.78v-1e1c-25.9 0-44 15.12-44 36.78v47.22h44v-42h-34v-5.22c0-18.5 17.08-26.78 34-26.78z"></path></svg><span class=button-link-text>Cite</span></button></div></div></div></div></div><div class="ArticleIdentifierLinks u-margin-xs-bottom text-xs" id=article-identifier-links><a class="anchor doi anchor-default anchor-external-link" href=https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jallcom.2003.11.172 target=_blank rel="noreferrer noopener" aria-label="Persistent link using digital object identifier" title="Persistent link using digital object identifier"><span class=anchor-text>https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jallcom.2003.11.172</span></a><a class="anchor rights-and-content anchor-default anchor-external-link" href="https://s100.copyright.com/AppDispatchServlet?publisherName=ELS&amp;contentID=S0925838804001495&amp;orderBeanReset=true" target=_blank rel="noreferrer noopener"><span class=anchor-text>Get rights and content</span></a></div><section class=ReferencedArticles></section><section class=ReferencedArticles></section><div class=PageDivider></div><div class="Abstracts u-font-gulliver text-s" id=abstracts><div class="abstract author" id=aep-abstract-id11><h2 class="section-title u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">Abstract</h2><div id=aep-abstract-sec-id12><p>Deformation behaviour of the Mg–0.7&nbsp;wt.% Nd alloy was investigated in tensile tests at elevated temperatures. Obtained local maximum in the temperature dependence of the yield stress as well as in the maximum stress indicates a presence of the dynamic strain ageing. The Portevin–Le Chatelier effect was observed at temperatures between 200 and 300&nbsp;°C. In the microstructure of deformed samples many twins were observed. The twinning mode of deformation decreases with increasing test temperature.</p></div></div></div><ul id=issue-navigation class="issue-navigation u-margin-s-bottom u-bg-grey1"><li class="previous move-left u-padding-s-ver u-padding-s-left"><a class="button-alternative button-alternative-tertiary" href=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0925838804001483><svg role=img focusable=false viewBox="0 0 54 128" width=10.125 height=24 class="icon icon-navigate-left u-fill-grey8" aria-label="Navigate Left"><path d="m1 61l45-45 7 7-38 38 38 38-7 7z"></path></svg><span class=button-alternative-text>Previous <span class=extra-detail-1>article</span><span class=extra-detail-2> in issue</span></span></a><li class="next move-right u-padding-s-ver u-padding-s-right"><a class="button-alternative button-alternative-tertiary" href=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0925838804001501><span class=button-alternative-text>Next <span class=extra-detail-1>article</span><span class=extra-detail-2> in issue</span></span><svg role=img focusable=false viewBox="0 0 54 128" width=10.125 height=24 class="icon icon-navigate-right u-fill-grey8" aria-label="Navigate Right"><path d="m1 99l38-38-38-38 7-7 45 45-45 45z"></path></svg></a></ul><div class="Keywords u-font-gulliver text-s"><div id=aep-keywords-id13 class=keywords-section><h2 class="section-title u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">Keywords</h2><div class=keyword><span>Metals</span></div><div class=keyword><span>Casting</span></div><div class=keyword><span>Dislocations</span></div><div class=keyword><span>Point defects</span></div><div class=keyword><span>Strain</span></div></div></div><div class="Body u-font-gulliver text-s" id=body><div><section id=aep-section-id14><h2 class="u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">1. Introduction</h2><p>It is well known that rare earths have favourable influence on mechanical properties of magnesium alloys especially at higher temperatures <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#BIB1 name=bBIB1><span class=anchor-text>[1]</span></a>, <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#BIB2 name=bBIB2><span class=anchor-text>[2]</span></a>. High performance magnesium rare earth alloys offer considerable advantages to the aerospace and racing automotive industry <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#BIB1 name=bBIB1><span class=anchor-text>[1]</span></a>. Key properties of these alloys include high specific strength at room and elevated temperatures, good creep resistance and the excellent castability and damping characteristics. However, despite if use of magnesium rare earth alloys for approaching 50 years, the scientific understanding of the influence of rare earth on mechanical properties is incomplete.<p>The aim of this work is to investigate the deformation behaviour of the Mg–0.7&nbsp;wt.% Nd at elevated temperatures and to account for the results obtained.</p></section><section id=aep-section-id15><h2 class="u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">2. Experimental procedures</h2><p>Magnesium of commercial purity with 0.7&nbsp;wt.% Nd was used in this study. Cylindrical specimens with 5&nbsp;mm of diameter and 25&nbsp;mm of length for tensile tests were prepared from squeeze cast ingots. Thermal treatment <em>T</em>6 (homogenisation at 525&nbsp;°C for 5&nbsp;h, then quenching into water 65&nbsp;°C warm with subsequent precipitation treating for 8&nbsp;h at 204&nbsp;°C). Deformation tests were performed in an Instron machine in the temperature interval from the room temperature to 300&nbsp;°C at an initial strain rate from 5.5×10<sup>−5</sup> to 1.1×10<sup>−3</sup>&nbsp;s<sup>−1</sup>. Microstructure of deformed and undeformed samples was observed by Olympus light microscope.</p></section><section id=aep-section-id16><h2 class="u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">3. Results and discussion</h2><div><p>The <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#FIG1 name=bFIG1><span class=anchor-text>Fig. 1</span></a> shows a representative microstructure of undeformed samples after <em>T</em>6 temperature. Grains in the microstructure are formed by the hexagonal close packed (hcp) solid solution of Nd in Mg with particles inside the grains and decorating the grain boundaries. Solubility of Nd in Mg is small. Park and Wyman <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#BIB3 name=bBIB3><span class=anchor-text>[3]</span></a> placed the maximum at 0.1&nbsp;at.% at the eutectic temperature. According to <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#BIB4 name=bBIB4><span class=anchor-text>[4]</span></a> the precipitation process occurs in Mg–Nd alloy as follows: solid solution → GP zones → β″ Mg<sub>3</sub>Nd cph phase → β′ Mg<sub>3</sub>Nd (fcc) → β Mg<sub>12</sub>Nd phase. The addition of Nd in dilute amounts to Mg results in significant increases on hardening and strength after adequate thermal treatment <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#BIB5 name=bBIB5><span class=anchor-text>[5]</span></a>. This increase in the hardness was reported due to formation of plate-shaped GP zones and precipitates on prismatic planes of the Mg matrix <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#BIB6 name=bBIB6><span class=anchor-text>[6]</span></a>. While structure of the precipitate plates (designated as β″) was commonly reported to be D0<sub>19</sub> superlattice the habit plane of the precipitate plates has been reported to be parallel to either <span class=math><span class=MathJax_Preview></span><span class=MathJax_SVG id=MathJax-Element-1-Frame tabindex=0 style=font-size:90%;display:inline-block;position:relative data-mathml='<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mtext>{1</mtext><mspace sp="0.25" width="2px" linebreak="nobreak" is="true" /><mtext>1</mtext><mspace sp="0.25" width="2px" linebreak="nobreak" is="true" /><mtext>2</mtext><mtext>&amp;#x304;</mtext><mspace sp="0.25" width="2px" linebreak="nobreak" is="true" /><mtext>0}</mtext><msub><mi></mi><mn><mtext>&amp;#x3B1;</mtext></mn></msub></math>' role=presentation><svg xmlns:xlink=http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink width=8.952ex height=2.779ex viewBox="0 -846.5 3854.5 1196.3" role=img focusable=false style=vertical-align:-0.812ex aria-hidden=true><g stroke=currentColor fill=currentColor stroke-width=0 transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><use href=#MJMAIN-7B></use><use href=#MJMAIN-31 x=500 y=0></use><g sp=0.25 is=true></g><use href=#MJMAIN-31 x=1100 y=0></use><g sp=0.25 is=true></g><use href=#MJMAIN-32 x=1700 y=0></use><use href=#MJMAIN-304 x=2200 y=0></use><g sp=0.25 is=true></g><g transform=translate(2300,0)><use href=#MJMAIN-30></use><use href=#MJMAIN-7D x=500 y=0></use></g><g transform=translate(3301,0)><use transform=scale(0.707) href=#MJMATHI-3B1 x=0 y=-213></use></g></g></svg><span class=MJX_Assistive_MathML role=presentation><math xmlns=http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML><mtext>{1</mtext><mspace sp=0.25 width=2px linebreak=nobreak is=true></mspace><mtext>1</mtext><mspace sp=0.25 width=2px linebreak=nobreak is=true></mspace><mtext>2</mtext><mtext>̄</mtext><mspace sp=0.25 width=2px linebreak=nobreak is=true></mspace><mtext>0}</mtext><msub><mi></mi><mn><mtext>α</mtext></mn></msub></math></span></span></span> or <span class=math><span class=MathJax_Preview></span><span class=MathJax_SVG id=MathJax-Element-2-Frame tabindex=0 style=font-size:90%;display:inline-block;position:relative data-mathml='<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mtext>{1</mtext><mspace sp="0.25" width="2px" linebreak="nobreak" is="true" /><mtext>0</mtext><mspace sp="0.25" width="2px" linebreak="nobreak" is="true" /><mtext>1</mtext><mtext>&amp;#x304;</mtext><mspace sp="0.25" width="2px" linebreak="nobreak" is="true" /><mtext>0}</mtext><msub><mi></mi><mn><mtext>&amp;#x3B1;</mtext></mn></msub></math>' role=presentation><svg xmlns:xlink=http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink width=8.952ex height=2.779ex viewBox="0 -846.5 3854.5 1196.3" role=img focusable=false style=vertical-align:-0.812ex aria-hidden=true><g stroke=currentColor fill=currentColor stroke-width=0 transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><use href=#MJMAIN-7B></use><use href=#MJMAIN-31 x=500 y=0></use><g sp=0.25 is=true></g><use href=#MJMAIN-30 x=1100 y=0></use><g sp=0.25 is=true></g><use href=#MJMAIN-31 x=1700 y=0></use><use href=#MJMAIN-304 x=2200 y=0></use><g sp=0.25 is=true></g><g transform=translate(2300,0)><use href=#MJMAIN-30></use><use href=#MJMAIN-7D x=500 y=0></use></g><g transform=translate(3301,0)><use transform=scale(0.707) href=#MJMATHI-3B1 x=0 y=-213></use></g></g></svg><span class=MJX_Assistive_MathML role=presentation><math xmlns=http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML><mtext>{1</mtext><mspace sp=0.25 width=2px linebreak=nobreak is=true></mspace><mtext>0</mtext><mspace sp=0.25 width=2px linebreak=nobreak is=true></mspace><mtext>1</mtext><mtext>̄</mtext><mspace sp=0.25 width=2px linebreak=nobreak is=true></mspace><mtext>0}</mtext><msub><mi></mi><mn><mtext>α</mtext></mn></msub></math></span></span></span> planes of the matrix phase. The GP zones were reported to have a faulted D0<sub>19</sub> structure and to form on <span class=math><span class=MathJax_Preview></span><span class=MathJax_SVG id=MathJax-Element-3-Frame tabindex=0 style=font-size:90%;display:inline-block;position:relative data-mathml='<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mtext>{1</mtext><mspace sp="0.25" width="2px" linebreak="nobreak" is="true" /><mtext>0</mtext><mspace sp="0.25" width="2px" linebreak="nobreak" is="true" /><mtext>1</mtext><mtext>&amp;#x304;</mtext><mspace sp="0.25" width="2px" linebreak="nobreak" is="true" /><mtext>0}</mtext><msub><mi></mi><mn><mtext>&amp;#x3B1;</mtext></mn></msub></math>' role=presentation><svg xmlns:xlink=http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink width=8.952ex height=2.779ex viewBox="0 -846.5 3854.5 1196.3" role=img focusable=false style=vertical-align:-0.812ex aria-hidden=true><g stroke=currentColor fill=currentColor stroke-width=0 transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><use href=#MJMAIN-7B></use><use href=#MJMAIN-31 x=500 y=0></use><g sp=0.25 is=true></g><use href=#MJMAIN-30 x=1100 y=0></use><g sp=0.25 is=true></g><use href=#MJMAIN-31 x=1700 y=0></use><use href=#MJMAIN-304 x=2200 y=0></use><g sp=0.25 is=true></g><g transform=translate(2300,0)><use href=#MJMAIN-30></use><use href=#MJMAIN-7D x=500 y=0></use></g><g transform=translate(3301,0)><use transform=scale(0.707) href=#MJMATHI-3B1 x=0 y=-213></use></g></g></svg><span class=MJX_Assistive_MathML role=presentation><math xmlns=http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML><mtext>{1</mtext><mspace sp=0.25 width=2px linebreak=nobreak is=true></mspace><mtext>0</mtext><mspace sp=0.25 width=2px linebreak=nobreak is=true></mspace><mtext>1</mtext><mtext>̄</mtext><mspace sp=0.25 width=2px linebreak=nobreak is=true></mspace><mtext>0}</mtext><msub><mi></mi><mn><mtext>α</mtext></mn></msub></math></span></span></span> planes of the matrix phase <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#BIB5 name=bBIB5><span class=anchor-text>[5]</span></a>, <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#BIB6 name=bBIB6><span class=anchor-text>[6]</span></a>.<figure class="figure text-xs" id=FIG1><span><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," height=354 alt><ol class=u-margin-s-bottom><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans u-display-inline anchor-default" href=https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S0925838804001495-gr1.jpg target=_blank download title="Download full-size image"><span class=anchor-text>Download : <span class=download-link-title>Download full-size image</span></span></a></ol></span><span class="captions text-s"><span><p><span class=label>Fig. 1</span>. Microstructure of Mg–Nd alloy after <em>T</em>6 temperature.</p></span></span></figure></div><div><p>The true stress–true strain curves obtained at various temperatures are introduced in <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#FIG2 name=bFIG2><span class=anchor-text>Fig. 2</span></a>. From <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#FIG2 name=bFIG2><span class=anchor-text>Fig. 2</span></a> it is obvious that strain hardening was observed in the whole temperature interval studied. Ductility of the alloy is relatively low even at elevated temperatures. Serrated yielding was observed at temperatures 250–300&nbsp;°C at the strain rate of <span class=math><span class=MathJax_Preview></span><span class=MathJax_SVG id=MathJax-Element-4-Frame tabindex=0 style=font-size:90%;display:inline-block;position:relative data-mathml='<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mtext>&amp;#x3B5;</mtext><mtext>&amp;#x307;</mtext><mtext>=3.3&amp;#xD7;10</mtext><msup><mi></mi><mn>&amp;#x2212;4</mn></msup><mspace sp="0.25" width="2px" linebreak="nobreak" is="true" /><mtext>s</mtext><msup><mi></mi><mn>&amp;#x2212;1</mn></msup></math>' role=presentation><svg xmlns:xlink=http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink width=15.81ex height=2.317ex viewBox="0 -896.2 6807.1 997.6" role=img focusable=false style=vertical-align:-0.235ex aria-hidden=true><g stroke=currentColor fill=currentColor stroke-width=0 transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><use href=#MJMATHI-3B5 x=0 y=0></use><use href=#MJMAIN-307 x=466 y=0></use><g transform=translate(467,0)><use href=#MJMAIN-3D></use><use href=#MJMAIN-33 x=778 y=0></use><use href=#MJMAIN-2E x=1279 y=0></use><use href=#MJMAIN-33 x=1557 y=0></use><use href=#MJMAIN-D7 x=2058 y=0></use><use href=#MJMAIN-31 x=2836 y=0></use><use href=#MJMAIN-30 x=3337 y=0></use></g><g transform=translate(4304,0)><g transform=translate(0,362)><use transform=scale(0.707) href=#MJMAIN-2212></use><use transform=scale(0.707) href=#MJMAIN-34 x=778 y=0></use></g></g><g sp=0.25 is=true></g><use href=#MJMAIN-73 x=5408 y=0></use><g transform=translate(5802,0)><g transform=translate(0,362)><use transform=scale(0.707) href=#MJMAIN-2212></use><use transform=scale(0.707) href=#MJMAIN-31 x=778 y=0></use></g></g></g></svg><span class=MJX_Assistive_MathML role=presentation><math xmlns=http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML><mtext>ε</mtext><mtext>̇</mtext><mtext>=3.3×10</mtext><msup><mi></mi><mn>−4</mn></msup><mspace sp=0.25 width=2px linebreak=nobreak is=true></mspace><mtext>s</mtext><msup><mi></mi><mn>−1</mn></msup></math></span></span></span>. Temperature dependences of the yield stress as well as the maximum stress are introduced in <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#FIG3 name=bFIG3><span class=anchor-text>Fig. 3</span></a>. It can be seen that the course of the temperature dependence is non-monotonous. A local maximum in the temperature dependence of both the yield stress and the maximum stress is observed in the temperature interval of 200–250&nbsp;°C.<figure class="figure text-xs" id=FIG2><span><img src=data:image/gif;base64, height=205 alt><ol class=u-margin-s-bottom><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans u-display-inline anchor-default" href=https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S0925838804001495-gr2.gif target=_blank download title="Download full-size image"><span class=anchor-text>Download : <span class=download-link-title>Download full-size image</span></span></a></ol></span><span class="captions text-s"><span><p><span class=label>Fig. 2</span>. Stress–strain curves obtained at various temperatures, initial strain rate 3.3×10<sup>−4</sup>&nbsp;s<sup>−1</sup>.</p></span></span></figure><figure class="figure text-xs" id=FIG3><span><img src="data:image/gif;base64," height=202 alt><ol class=u-margin-s-bottom><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans u-display-inline anchor-default" href=https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S0925838804001495-gr3.gif target=_blank download title="Download full-size image"><span class=anchor-text>Download : <span class=download-link-title>Download full-size image</span></span></a></ol></span><span class="captions text-s"><span><p><span class=label>Fig. 3</span>. Temperature dependence of the maximum stress and the yield stress.</p></span></span></figure></div><div><p>Characteristic feature of the microstructure after deformation is a lot of deformation twins (<a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#FIG4 name=bFIG4><span class=anchor-text>Fig. 4a,b</span></a>). Twins were observed in whole temperature interval studied, their density decreased with increasing temperature. Twins as the important deformation mode were observed by Máthis et al. in the case of AM60 magnesium alloy <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#BIB7 name=bBIB7><span class=anchor-text>[7]</span></a>. The formation of twins contributes also to deformation. Twinning may reorient the basal planes that become more favourable for slip. Zhang et al. <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#BIB8 name=bBIB8><span class=anchor-text>[8]</span></a> have reported this behaviour. The twinning planes are <span class=math><span class=MathJax_Preview></span><span class=MathJax_SVG id=MathJax-Element-5-Frame tabindex=0 style=font-size:90%;display:inline-block;position:relative data-mathml='<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mtext>{1</mtext><mspace sp="0.25" width="2px" linebreak="nobreak" is="true" /><mtext>0</mtext><mspace sp="0.25" width="2px" linebreak="nobreak" is="true" /><mtext>1</mtext><mtext>&amp;#x304;</mtext><mspace sp="0.25" width="2px" linebreak="nobreak" is="true" /><mtext>2}</mtext></math>' role=presentation><svg xmlns:xlink=http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink width=7.668ex height=2.779ex viewBox="0 -846.5 3301.6 1196.3" role=img focusable=false style=vertical-align:-0.812ex aria-hidden=true><g stroke=currentColor fill=currentColor stroke-width=0 transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><use href=#MJMAIN-7B></use><use href=#MJMAIN-31 x=500 y=0></use><g sp=0.25 is=true></g><use href=#MJMAIN-30 x=1100 y=0></use><g sp=0.25 is=true></g><use href=#MJMAIN-31 x=1700 y=0></use><use href=#MJMAIN-304 x=2200 y=0></use><g sp=0.25 is=true></g><g transform=translate(2300,0)><use href=#MJMAIN-32></use><use href=#MJMAIN-7D x=500 y=0></use></g></g></svg><span class=MJX_Assistive_MathML role=presentation><math xmlns=http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML><mtext>{1</mtext><mspace sp=0.25 width=2px linebreak=nobreak is=true></mspace><mtext>0</mtext><mspace sp=0.25 width=2px linebreak=nobreak is=true></mspace><mtext>1</mtext><mtext>̄</mtext><mspace sp=0.25 width=2px linebreak=nobreak is=true></mspace><mtext>2}</mtext></math></span></span></span> and <span class=math><span class=MathJax_Preview></span><span class=MathJax_SVG id=MathJax-Element-6-Frame tabindex=0 style=font-size:90%;display:inline-block;position:relative data-mathml='<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mtext>{3</mtext><mspace sp="0.25" width="2px" linebreak="nobreak" is="true" /><mtext>0</mtext><mspace sp="0.25" width="2px" linebreak="nobreak" is="true" /><mtext>3</mtext><mtext>&amp;#x304;</mtext><mspace sp="0.25" width="2px" linebreak="nobreak" is="true" /><mtext>4}</mtext></math>' role=presentation><svg xmlns:xlink=http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink width=7.668ex height=2.779ex viewBox="0 -846.5 3301.6 1196.3" role=img focusable=false style=vertical-align:-0.812ex aria-hidden=true><g stroke=currentColor fill=currentColor stroke-width=0 transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><use href=#MJMAIN-7B></use><use href=#MJMAIN-33 x=500 y=0></use><g sp=0.25 is=true></g><use href=#MJMAIN-30 x=1100 y=0></use><g sp=0.25 is=true></g><use href=#MJMAIN-33 x=1700 y=0></use><use href=#MJMAIN-304 x=2200 y=0></use><g sp=0.25 is=true></g><g transform=translate(2300,0)><use href=#MJMAIN-34></use><use href=#MJMAIN-7D x=500 y=0></use></g></g></svg><span class=MJX_Assistive_MathML role=presentation><math xmlns=http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML><mtext>{3</mtext><mspace sp=0.25 width=2px linebreak=nobreak is=true></mspace><mtext>0</mtext><mspace sp=0.25 width=2px linebreak=nobreak is=true></mspace><mtext>3</mtext><mtext>̄</mtext><mspace sp=0.25 width=2px linebreak=nobreak is=true></mspace><mtext>4}</mtext></math></span></span></span> but at higher temperatures twins were observed in plane <span class=math><span class=MathJax_Preview></span><span class=MathJax_SVG id=MathJax-Element-7-Frame tabindex=0 style=font-size:90%;display:inline-block;position:relative data-mathml='<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mtext>{1</mtext><mspace sp="0.25" width="2px" linebreak="nobreak" is="true" /><mtext>0</mtext><mspace sp="0.25" width="2px" linebreak="nobreak" is="true" /><mtext>1</mtext><mtext>&amp;#x304;</mtext><mspace sp="0.25" width="2px" linebreak="nobreak" is="true" /><mtext>3}</mtext></math>' role=presentation><svg xmlns:xlink=http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink width=7.668ex height=2.779ex viewBox="0 -846.5 3301.6 1196.3" role=img focusable=false style=vertical-align:-0.812ex aria-hidden=true><g stroke=currentColor fill=currentColor stroke-width=0 transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><use href=#MJMAIN-7B></use><use href=#MJMAIN-31 x=500 y=0></use><g sp=0.25 is=true></g><use href=#MJMAIN-30 x=1100 y=0></use><g sp=0.25 is=true></g><use href=#MJMAIN-31 x=1700 y=0></use><use href=#MJMAIN-304 x=2200 y=0></use><g sp=0.25 is=true></g><g transform=translate(2300,0)><use href=#MJMAIN-33></use><use href=#MJMAIN-7D x=500 y=0></use></g></g></svg><span class=MJX_Assistive_MathML role=presentation><math xmlns=http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML><mtext>{1</mtext><mspace sp=0.25 width=2px linebreak=nobreak is=true></mspace><mtext>0</mtext><mspace sp=0.25 width=2px linebreak=nobreak is=true></mspace><mtext>1</mtext><mtext>̄</mtext><mspace sp=0.25 width=2px linebreak=nobreak is=true></mspace><mtext>3}</mtext></math></span></span></span>, too <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#BIB9 name=bBIB9><span class=anchor-text>[9]</span></a>.<figure class="figure text-xs" id=FIG4><span><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," height=798 alt><ol class=u-margin-s-bottom><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans u-display-inline anchor-default" href=https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S0925838804001495-gr4.jpg target=_blank download title="Download full-size image"><span class=anchor-text>Download : <span class=download-link-title>Download full-size image</span></span></a></ol></span><span class="captions text-s"><span><p><span class=label>Fig. 4</span>. Twins observed after deformation at (a) 100&nbsp;°C and (b) 300&nbsp;°C.</p></span></span></figure></div><div><p>Serrated character of stress–strain curves obtained at 200–300&nbsp;°C can be caused by either twinning or the Portevin–LeChâtelier (PLC) effect. To decide this problem stress–strain curves were performed at 250&nbsp;°C and various strain rates from 5.5×10<sup>−5</sup> to 1.1×10<sup>−3</sup>&nbsp;s<sup>−1</sup>. The strain rate dependence of the yield stress is introduced in <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#FIG5 name=bFIG5><span class=anchor-text>Fig. 5</span></a>. Negative strain rate sensitivity of this dependence in the strain rate interval from 5.5×10<sup>−5</sup> to 5.5×10<sup>−4</sup>&nbsp;s<sup>−1</sup> indicates a presence of the unstable plastic deformation which is manifested by the PLC effect (see <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#FIG6 name=bFIG6><span class=anchor-text>Fig. 6</span></a>). From the microscopic point of view, it is widely accepted that the PLC effect arises from the solute diffusion assisted obstruction of dislocation motion. It appears when the solute mobility is comparable with the dislocation velocity. The solute concentration increases generally around the cores of mobile dislocations exhibiting repeated transitions between the enriched state and the saturated one and vice versa, which corresponds to the slow and fast deformation regime, respectively. The microscopic models of PLC effect were developed in several papers <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#BIB10 name=bBIB10><span class=anchor-text>[10]</span></a>, <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#BIB11 name=bBIB11><span class=anchor-text>[11]</span></a>, <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#BIB12 name=bBIB12><span class=anchor-text>[12]</span></a>, <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#BIB13 name=bBIB13><span class=anchor-text>[13]</span></a>, <a class="anchor workspace-trigger u-display-inline anchor-paragraph" href=#BIB14 name=bBIB14><span class=anchor-text>[14]</span></a>. PLC effect requires some critical density of dislocations and critical waiting time for dislocations pinned at obstacles. It is also reason why the PLC effect apears at some critical strain and in the relatively narrow strain rate interval. Movable solute atoms are necessary for the occurrence of the PLC effect. Although the alloy studied obeyed to <em>T</em>6 temperature it has very probably sufficient number of solute atoms for the occurence of such mechanism.<figure class="figure text-xs" id=FIG5><span><img src="data:image/gif;base64," height=236 alt><ol class=u-margin-s-bottom><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans u-display-inline anchor-default" href=https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S0925838804001495-gr5.gif target=_blank download title="Download full-size image"><span class=anchor-text>Download : <span class=download-link-title>Download full-size image</span></span></a></ol></span><span class="captions text-s"><span><p><span class=label>Fig. 5</span>. Stress–strain curves obtained at various strain rates at 250&nbsp;°C.</p></span></span></figure><figure class="figure text-xs" id=FIG6><span><img src="data:image/gif;base64," height=237 alt><ol class=u-margin-s-bottom><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans u-display-inline anchor-default" href=https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S0925838804001495-gr6.gif target=_blank download title="Download full-size image"><span class=anchor-text>Download : <span class=download-link-title>Download full-size image</span></span></a></ol></span><span class="captions text-s"><span><p><span class=label>Fig. 6</span>. Strain rate dependence of the yield stress.</p></span></span></figure></div></section><section id=aep-section-id17><h2 class="u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">4. Conclusions</h2><p>Deformation behaviour of Mg–0.7Nd alloy was studied at elevated temperatures. Temperature dependence of the yield stress as well as the maximum stress is non-monotonous. Local maximum between 200–250&nbsp;°C is a consequence of dynamic strain ageing in the alloy. The second demonstration of the strain ageing phenomenon is the presence of the Portevin–LeChâtelier effect which was observed in the temperature interval from 200–300&nbsp;°C. The negative slope in the strain rate dependence of the yield stress is the evidence of the PLC effect. Free solution atoms in the solid solution are the reason for strain ageing effects. Twinning deformation mode was observed in the whole temperature interval and its influence decreases with increasing test temperature.</p></section></div><section id=aep-acknowledgment-id18><h2 class="u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">Acknowledgements</h2><p>This investigation was financially supported by the Grant Agency of the Academy of Sciences of the Czech Republic under grant A2041203.</p></section></div><div class="related-content-links u-hide-from-md sf-hidden"></div><div class="Tail text-s"></div><section class="bibliography u-font-gulliver text-s" id=aep-bibliography-id19><h2 class="section-title u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">References</h2><section class=bibliography-sec id=aep-bibliography-sec-id20><dl class=references id=reference-links-aep-bibliography-sec-id20><dt class="label u-font-sans"><a class="anchor anchor-default" href=#bBIB1 id=ref-id-BIB1 data-aa-button="sd:product:journal:article:location=references:type=anchor:name=citation-name"><span class=anchor-text>[1]</span></a><dd class=reference><div class=other-ref><span>J.F. King, in: G.B. Brook (Ed.), Advances in Material Technology International, Sterling, 1990, p. 12.</span></div><div class="ReferenceLinks u-font-sans"><a class="anchor link anchor-default anchor-external-link" href="https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=J.F.%20King,%20in:%20G.B.%20Brook%20,%20A