:article:location=citing-articles:type=view-details" aria-describedby=citing-articles-article-0-title aria-controls=citing-articles-article-0 aria-expanded=false type=button><span class=button-link-text>Show abstract</span><svg role=img focusable=false viewBox="0 0 92 128" width=17.25 height=24 class="icon icon-navigate-down" aria-label="Navigate Down"><path d="m1 51l7-7 38 38 38-38 7 7-45 45z"></path></svg></button></div><div class="u-display-none sf-hidden" aria-hidden=true></div><li class="ListArticleItem u-margin-l-bottom"><div class="sub-heading u-margin-xs-bottom"><h3 class=u-font-gulliver id=citing-articles-article-1-title><a class="anchor anchor-default" href=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0925838818302329 target=_self><span class=anchor-text>Effects of rare earth yttrium on microstructure and properties of Mg–Al–Zn alloy</span></a></h3><div class=text-s>2018, Journal of Alloys and Compounds</div><div class="CitedSection u-margin-s-top"><div class=u-margin-s-left><div class="cite-header text-s u-text-italic u-font-sans">Citation Excerpt :</div><p class="u-font-gulliver text-xs">Fig. 2 shows that minor Y element added to the alloy 1 facilitates the formation of the Al2Y phases. Y element, which is solid-soluble in the α-Mg matrix, causes solid solution strengthening [28,29], reduces the number of dislocation plugs and stress concentration lighter, and therefore improves the hardness. However, when the Y content exceeds 0.25 mass.</p></div></div></div><div class="buttons text-s"><button class="button-link button-link-secondary" data-aa-button="sd:product:journal:article:location=citing-articles:type=view-details" aria-describedby=citing-articles-article-1-title aria-controls=citing-articles-article-1 aria-expanded=false type=button><span class=button-link-text>Show abstract</span><svg role=img focusable=false viewBox="0 0 92 128" width=17.25 height=24 class="icon icon-navigate-down" aria-label="Navigate Down"><path d="m1 51l7-7 38 38 38-38 7 7-45 45z"></path></svg></button></div><div class="u-display-none sf-hidden" aria-hidden=true></div><li class="ListArticleItem u-margin-l-bottom"><div class="sub-heading u-margin-xs-bottom"><h3 class=u-font-gulliver id=citing-articles-article-2-title><a class="anchor anchor-default" href=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1044580315002910 target=_self><span class=anchor-text>Microstructure evolution during heat treatment and mechanical properties of Mg-2.49Nd-1.82Gd-0.19Zn-0.4Zr cast alloy</span></a></h3><div class=text-s>2015, Materials Characterization</div><div class="CitedSection u-margin-s-top"><div class=u-margin-s-left><div class="cite-header text-s u-text-italic u-font-sans">Citation Excerpt :</div><p class="u-font-gulliver text-xs">It has been reported that the addition of Nd with an amount of 0.21 to 2.65 wt. % in Mg can significantly increase the hardness and yield strength (YS) after adequate thermal treatments because of the formation of “Guinier–Preston, GP” zones and β′ (Mg3Nd) phase [3,4]. Moreover, a trace addition of Zn and Zr elements into Mg–Nd based alloys also enhances the mechanical properties.</p></div></div></div><div class="buttons text-s"><button class="button-link button-link-secondary" data-aa-button="sd:product:journal:article:location=citing-articles:type=view-details" aria-describedby=citing-articles-article-2-title aria-controls=citing-articles-article-2 aria-expanded=false type=button><span class=button-link-text>Show abstract</span><svg role=img focusable=false viewBox="0 0 92 128" width=17.25 height=24 class="icon icon-navigate-down" aria-label="Navigate Down"><path d="m1 51l7-7 38 38 38-38 7 7-45 45z"></path></svg></button></div><div class="u-display-none sf-hidden" aria-hidden=true></div><li class="ListArticleItem u-margin-l-bottom"><div class="sub-heading u-margin-xs-bottom"><h3 class=u-font-gulliver id=citing-articles-article-3-title><a class="anchor anchor-default" href=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0921509312016590 target=_self><span class=anchor-text>Effects of cerium on microstructures, recovery behavior and mechanical properties of backward extruded Mg-0.5Mn alloys</span></a></h3><div class=text-s>2013, Materials Science and Engineering A</div><div class="CitedSection u-margin-s-top"><div class=u-margin-s-left><div class="cite-header text-s u-text-italic u-font-sans">Citation Excerpt :</div><p class="u-font-gulliver text-xs">It is commonly deemed that the formability of Mg alloys is strongly affected by the activity of non-basal slip, which is promoted by the reduction of c/a ratio [1]. Hence, the additions of Li, Th, Y, Nd, Ce and Gd have been employed to improve the deformability of Mg alloys [2,3]. However, Mg–Ce system exhibits some especial characteristics.</p></div></div></div><div class="buttons text-s"><button class="button-link button-link-secondary" data-aa-button="sd:product:journal:article:location=citing-articles:type=view-details" aria-describedby=citing-articles-article-3-title aria-controls=citing-articles-article-3 aria-expanded=false type=button><span class=button-link-text>Show abstract</span><svg role=img focusable=false viewBox="0 0 92 128" width=17.25 height=24 class="icon icon-navigate-down" aria-label="Navigate Down"><path d="m1 51l7-7 38 38 38-38 7 7-45 45z"></path></svg></button></div><div class="u-display-none sf-hidden" aria-hidden=true></div><li class="ListArticleItem u-margin-l-bottom"><div class="sub-heading u-margin-xs-bottom"><h3 class=u-font-gulliver id=citing-articles-article-4-title><a class="anchor anchor-default" href=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S092150931100270X target=_self><span class=anchor-text>Orientation dependencies of mechanical response, microstructure and texture evolution in hot compression of AZ31 magnesium alloy processed by equal channel angular extrusion</span></a></h3><div class=text-s>2011, Materials Science and Engineering A</div></div><div class="buttons text-s"><button class="button-link button-link-secondary" data-aa-button="sd:product:journal:article:location=citing-articles:type=view-details" aria-describedby=citing-articles-article-4-title aria-controls=citing-articles-article-4 aria-expanded=false type=button><span class=button-link-text>Show abstract</span><svg role=img focusable=false viewBox="0 0 92 128" width=17.25 height=24 class="icon icon-navigate-down" aria-label="Navigate Down"><path d="m1 51l7-7 38 38 38-38 7 7-45 45z"></path></svg></button></div><div class="u-display-none sf-hidden" aria-hidden=true></div><li class="ListArticleItem u-margin-l-bottom"><div class="sub-heading u-margin-xs-bottom"><h3 class=u-font-gulliver id=citing-articles-article-5-title><a class="anchor anchor-default" href=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0921509310002583 target=_self><span class=anchor-text>Forgeability and die-forging forming of direct chill casting Mg-Nd-Zn-Zr magnesium alloy</span></a></h3><div class=text-s>2010, Materials Science and Engineering A</div></div><div class="buttons text-s"><button class="button-link button-link-secondary" data-aa-button="sd:product:journal:article:location=citing-articles:type=view-details" aria-describedby=citing-articles-article-5-title aria-controls=citing-articles-article-5 aria-expanded=false type=button><span class=button-link-text>Show abstract</span><svg role=img focusable=false viewBox="0 0 92 128" width=17.25 height=24 class="icon icon-navigate-down" aria-label="Navigate Down"><path d="m1 51l7-7 38 38 38-38 7 7-45 45z"></path></svg></button></div><div class="u-display-none sf-hidden" aria-hidden=true></div></ul><a class="button-alternative button-alternative-secondary text-m" href="http://www.scopus.com/scopus/inward/citedby.url?partnerID=10&amp;rel=3.0.0&amp;eid=2-s2.0-4344683570&amp;md5=59cdad438c67e48e54714a8705b9240" id=citing-articles-view-all-btn target=_blank><svg role=img focusable=false viewBox="0 0 78 128" width=14.625 height=24 class="icon icon-arrow-up-